  "provenance": {
    "grade": "reconstructed",
    "basis": "Reconstructed standing depiction of Martha L. (Mattie) Williamson, the Glessners' cook (attested by the museum's account of her, written from Frances Glessner's journal: cook from 1892 to 1912 apart from a year away in 1897-98), standing in the Glessner House courtyard toward the carriage passage. She is the first woman drawn in the 1904 town (T-3423): MakeHuman's female-young macros, a pale shirtwaist with a standing collar, a dark skirt and a white bib apron cut after the working dress of the 1903 Wasson stereographs, hair drawn up to a knot. Her presence in the household is attested; where she stands, her dress, her hair, her face and her complexion are reconstructed, not observed.",
    "sources": [
      "glessner_house_blog_mattie_williamson_2020",
